{"id":"dfce69da-a6ce-4f15-8ffc-033406e0316d","object":"chat.completion","created":1784826636,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\":\"def solve(case):\\n    payload = case['payload']\\n    nodes = payload['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = set(payload['required_symbols'])\\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    children = {nid: [] for nid in node_map}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        pid = n.get('parent')\\n        if pid is not None:\\n            children[pid].append(n['id'])\\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    removal_order = []\\n    for n in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = n['id']\\n        if nid == 'root':\\n            continue\\n        if not n['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if n['symbol'] in required_symbols:\\n            continue\\n        has_retained_child = any(cid in retained for cid in children.get(nid, []))\\n        if has_retained_child:\\n            continue\\n        retained_now = retained - {nid}\\n        symbols_after = {node_map[nid2]['symbol'] for nid2 in retained_now}\\n        if required_symbols.issubset(symbols_after):\\n            retained = retained_now\\n            removal_order.append(nid)\\n    return {\\n        'retained': sorted(retained),\\n        'removal_order': removal_order\\n    }\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) that returns a JSON-serializable object. The task: \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\" based on a bounded objective. The case payload has nodes array (each node has id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility) and required_symbols list. The goal: reduce the parse tree by removing removable nodes while preserving required symbols and grammar validity? But grammar is not provided; we need to infer from context. The problem appears to be from the registered procedure \"SE-PE-01\". The description: \"Reduce a registered parse tree through grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates while preserving an interestingness oracle.\" However, the fixture only provides nodes and required_symbols. There is no grammar or interestingness oracle. The problem likely expects a simple reduction: remove nodes that are removable, not required, and whose removal doesn't affect required symbols (i.e., required symbols still appear in the tree after removal). But also need to consider \"deeper before shallower\", and \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" Also \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" And \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So we need to simulate a reduction algorithm.\n\nThe output should include \"sorted retained IDs\" and \"actual removal order\". Also likely need to return something like {\"retained\": [...], \"removal_order\": [...], ...}. But the problem statement says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So output should be an object with those fields.\n\nWe need to parse the case, build tree structure, then perform a traversal (depth-first, deeper first). For each node, if removable, not root, not required, and all children that are retained? Actually \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" So if node has any children that are not removed yet (or will be removed later? The algorithm: we process nodes in order, tentatively remove if possible. But if we remove a node, its children become orphans? The tree is hierarchical. Typically in parse tree reduction, removing a node means you replace it with its children (or delete it if it's a leaf?). But the description says \"tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" Also \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" That suggests you cannot remove a node that has children that are still in the tree (i.e., you can't delete an internal node if its children are kept). So you can only remove leaf nodes? But also nodes can be removable and have children if those children are also removed? The phrasing \"a node with retained children\" means children that are not removed. So if a node's children are all removed, then you can remove that node? Possibly. But it says \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" So you must remove children first (deeper nodes) before removing their parent. That aligns with \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" So we should process nodes in order of decreasing depth (deepest first), and for nodes at same depth, tie-break by node ID (stable). For each node, if it is removable, not root, not required, and its children are all already removed (or it has no children), and after removing it, the set of symbols in the tree still contains all required symbols? But wait: the required symbols are symbol names, not node IDs. So we need to ensure that every required symbol appears at least once among the retained nodes. If we remove a node, we lose its symbol. So we need to check that for each required symbol, there is at least one retained node (including possibly other nodes) with that symbol. So the algorithm: We maintain a set of retained nodes initially all nodes. Then we iterate nodes in order (deeper first, tie by ID). For each node, if it is removable, not root, not required, and all its children are not in retained set (i.e., already removed), and after tentatively removing it, the set of symbols of retained nodes (excluding this node) still covers all required symbols, then we remove it (add to removal order) and remove it from retained set.\n\nBut also need to consider \"grammar_valid\" – no grammar provided, so maybe we ignore. \"interestingness_preserved\" – not defined. So probably just the logical constraints.\n\nThus the solution: parse nodes into a dict by id. Build children list. Also compute parent mapping. Then create list of all node ids. Sort by depth descending, then by id ascending (stable tie break). Then iterate, apply removal condition. Keep track of retained set. Also note: the root is \"root\", symbol \"root\" is required? required_symbols includes \"root\". So root is not removable (removable false anyway). So we won't remove root.\n\nReturn: {\"retained\": sorted list of retained ids (sorted? probably by id? The problem says \"sorted retained IDs\" – likely sorted lexicographically or by id? We'll sort by id.), \"removal_order\": list of ids in order they were removed (actual removal order). Also maybe other fields? The problem says \"Return only JSON-serializable values with the exact named fields.\" So we need to decide fields. Possibly also \"utility\" or something? But it says \"sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So just those two fields. To be safe, we'll output {\"retained\": [...], \"removal_order\": [...]}. Also maybe \"reduction_success\" but not stated.\n\nWe must raise ValueError for infeasible cases (e.g., required symbols missing from start? But root is always there, so fine). Or if structure invalid.\n\nImplementation: solve(case) receives a dict with \"payload\": {\"nodes\": [...], \"required_symbols\": [...]}. We'll parse.\n\nNow write Python code. Ensure no mutation of case. Use only standard library. Return JSON-serializable (list of strings/ints). Node ids are strings like \"root\", \"n1\", etc. So retained and removal_order will be list of strings.\n\nWe'll implement inside solve().\n\nEdge cases: nodes with same depth but different IDs: tie break by ID string comparison? The problem says \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So sort by depth descending, then by id ascending (string comparison). That is fine.\n\nAlso note: \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" So if a node has children that are still retained (i.e., not removed yet), we cannot remove it. Since we process deepest first, children will be processed before parents, so if child is removable and gets removed, then parent may have no retained children later. So condition works.\n\nCheck: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That includes the symbol of the node itself? If the node's symbol is required, we already skip because removable false or required? Actually \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So required symbols are nodes whose symbol is in required_symbols? Or nodes with symbol that is required? The phrasing \"a required symbol\" likely means a node that has a symbol listed in required_symbols. So we should not remove any node whose symbol is in required_symbols. So even if removable true, we skip. So condition: node's symbol in required_symbols? But careful: multiple nodes can have same symbol. But if a required symbol appears multiple times, we only need to keep at least one. So maybe we can remove some nodes that have required symbol as long as another node with same symbol remains. But the instruction says \"Never remove ... a required symbol\" – that seems absolute: do not remove any node whose symbol is required. Because the specification says \"required_symbols\" – they are symbols that must remain in the tree. If you remove a node that has a required symbol, you might lose that symbol if no other node has it. But if there are multiple nodes with that symbol, you could remove one? The phrase \"required symbol\" could be interpreted as the symbol itself must be present, not that every node with that symbol must stay. However, the instruction says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" It groups them: root (node), required symbol (node with that symbol?), node with retained children. So I think they mean: do not remove a node that is the root, or a node whose symbol is in required_symbols, or a node that has retained children. So to be safe, we'll treat that as: if node['symbol'] in required_symbols, then do not remove it, regardless of other occurrences. Because the text explicitly says \"a required symbol\". But in the fixture, required_symbols includes \"root\" and \"s1\". The root node has symbol \"root\", so it's covered. Other nodes with symbol \"s1\" are also required? The fixture shows nodes with symbol \"s1\": n1, n4. Both have removable: n1 false, n4 true. So if we follow that rule, n4 cannot be removed because its symbol is required (s1). That matches the rule. But is that the intended behavior? Possibly yes, because the reduction must preserve all required symbols, meaning every occurrence? Or just at least one? The phrase \"required symbol\" might mean a token that must be present, but not necessarily every node. However, the rule \"Never remove ... a required symbol\" suggests you cannot remove a node that is a required symbol node. So we'll adopt that: if node.symbol in required_symbols, skip removal.\n\nBut then what about nodes with symbol not in required_symbols? They can be removed if removable and no retained children and after removal, all required symbols still present (since they are not removed, they are fine). Since we never remove required symbol nodes, they remain. So condition \"all required symbols remain represented\" is automatically satisfied. So the extra check is redundant but we can keep.\n\nThus algorithm: For each node in sorted order, if node.removable and node.symbol not in required_symbols and node.id != 'root' and no child in retained_set, then tentatively remove: remove from retained_set, add to removal_order.\n\nReturn retained_set sorted, removal_order.\n\nNow ensure we handle case where a node is not removable (removable==false) so skip.\n\nNow code.\n\nWe must not mutate input. So copy.\n\nWrite solve(case) as function.\n\nReturn dict.\n\nTest with provided fixtures: For case 0, nodes: root(removable false), n1(s1,rem false), n2(s2,rem true), n3(s0,rem false), n4(s1,rem true, parent n2), n5(s2,rem false, parent n3), n6(s0,rem true, parent n1). required_symbols: root, s1. So nodes with s1: root (root) actually root symbol is \"root\", not s1. So n1 (s1) and n4 (s1). Both are required symbol nodes, so we cannot remove them. So removable nodes: n2 (s2), n6 (s0). n2 has children? n4 is child of n2. n4 is retained (required). So n2 has a retained child, so cannot remove n2. n6 is child of n1, n1 retained, so n6 has no children? n6 is leaf? No children listed, so n6 has no children, so it's removable? But condition \"all required symbols remain represented\" – after removing n6, s1 still present (n1 and n4), root present, s0 gone but s0 not required. So n6 can be removed. Also n2 cannot because child retained. n4 is required so not removed. n5 is not removable. So only n6 removed. So retained: root, n1, n2, n3, n4, n5. Sorted: root, n1, n2, n3, n4, n5. Removal order: [n6]. That seems plausible.\n\nCheck case 1: similar, n6 removable? n6 parent n1, symbol s0, removable true, no children. n2 has child n4 (symbol s1 required) retained, so n2 not removable. n4 required symbol? yes s1, so not removable. So only n6 removed. So retained same set minus n6.\n\nCase 2: same.\n\nThus our algorithm yields consistent results.\n\nBut is that too trivial? The problem mentions grammar_guided_node_reduction and candidates. But with no grammar, we just do simple removal. Possibly the intended solution is exactly this.\n\nWe'll implement.\n\nNow produce JSON response with key \"implementation\" containing the Python source.\n\nMake sure to escape newlines and quotes.\n\nLet's write the code.\n\nI'll produce a compact but readable function.\n\nCode:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    payload = case['payload']\n    nodes = payload['nodes']\n    required_symbols = set(payload['required_symbols'])\n    # Build mapping\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\n    children = {nid: [] for nid in node_map}\n    for n in nodes:\n        pid = n.get('parent')\n        if pid is not None:\n            children[pid].append(n['id'])\n    # Initialize retained set\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\n    # Sort nodes by depth descending, then id ascending\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\n    removal_order = []\n    for n in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = n['id']\n        if nid == 'root':\n            continue\n        if not n['removable']:\n            continue\n        if n['symbol'] in required_symbols:\n            continue\n        # Check if any child is still retained\n        has_retained_child = any(cid in retained for cid in children.get(nid, []))\n        if has_retained_child:\n            continue\n        # Tentatively remove: check required symbols still present\n        # Since we never remove required symbol nodes, this is automatically true,\n        # but we check anyway for safety.\n        retained_now = retained - {nid}\n        symbols_after = {node_map[nid2]['symbol'] for nid2 in retained_now}\n        if required_symbols.issubset(symbols_after):\n            retained = retained_now\n            removal_order.append(nid)\n    return {\n        'retained': sorted(retained),\n        'removal_order': removal_order\n    }\n\nThat's it.\n\nNow we need to ensure we handle the case where a node's parent might be None (root). fine.\n\nNow produce JSON.\n\nMake sure to not use any external modules.\n\nNow write the final response."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1852,"completion_tokens":3466,"total_tokens":5318,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1792},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":3126},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1792,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":60},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}